1 EDIT

   Edits a file with an editor on the host, such as vi.  govax copies
   the file to a scratch directory on the host as lines of text, runs
   the editor on the copy, and if the editor wrote it, makes what it
   wrote the file's next version.  This command is govax's own: VMS's
   EDT and TPU aren't there.

   Format

     EDIT filespec

   SET EDITOR says which editor runs, and where its copies go.

   EDIT/SUM is VMS's SUMSLP batch editor (see /SUM).

2 Parameter

 filespec

   One file.  If no file has the name, and the name has no version, EDIT
   makes a new one (variable-length records, carriage return carriage
   control) if the editor writes it.

2 Description

   The new version has the record format, record attributes, protection,
   and owner of the version edited, so each line becomes a record of the
   same kind:

     Fixed      A short line is filled out with blanks.  The editor
                doesn't see the blanks at the end of a record.
     Variable,  The line is the record.
     stream
     VFC        The line is the record, after control bytes: those all
                the records had, or if they differed, the usual ones for
                the file, with a warning.

   A line longer than a fixed record, or than a file's maximum record
   size, loses the blanks at its end if that is enough.  Otherwise it is
   EDIT-E-TOOLONG, and there is no new version.

   The file is left alone if the editor doesn't write its copy (:q! in
   vi) or ends with a failure status (:cq in vi).  When the editor wrote
   its copy but that can't become a new version, the message ends with
   -EDIT-I-KEPT and the copy's name; the copy stays until you delete it.

   A file that isn't lines of text is refused before the editor starts:
   a directory, a relative or indexed file, one with undefined records,
   or one whose records hold a null byte or a line feed.  So is a file
   on a volume mounted read-only.

   Every process waits while the editor runs.

2 Qualifiers
/HOST

   The file is a host file, replaced when the editor has written its
   copy.

2 Examples

   1.$ EDIT LOGIN.COM

     Edits LOGIN.COM in the default directory, making a new version.

   2.$ SET EDITOR/COMMAND="vim -n %file"
     $ EDIT "notes.txt"/HOST

     Edits a host file with vim.

2 /SUM

   Runs SUMSLP, a batch editor: one input file is updated by the edit
   commands in one or more update files, and the result written as a
   new file.  The input and update files aren't changed.  Each update
   file's line numbers are the input's, so several can be merged into
   one list of edits and applied together.

   Format

     EDIT/SUM input-file/UPDATE[=(update-file[,...])]

3 Parameter

 input-file

   The file to update; wildcards aren't allowed.

3 Description

   An update file is lines of text.  A line that starts with one of
   these characters does something; any other line is a record to
   insert:

     -m,n    Lines m to n of the input are replaced by the records
             that follow (deleted, if none do).
     -m      The records that follow are inserted after line m (after
             no line, before line 1, for -0 or a bare -).
     /       The update file ends here.
     \       The audit trail is off for the lines after.
     %       The audit trail is on again.
     <       The next character is an ordinary one: </ is a record
             that starts with a slash.

   An edit command may end with ,/audit-trail/ (the text that marks the
   inserted lines in the listing) and ;comment.  A line number may be .,
   the line the last edit reached.

   With several update files, the last listed takes precedence where
   their edits meet.  SUMSLP's warnings (edits clash, edits out of
   sequence, premature end-of-file, SLP command syntax error) go into
   the listing, or to SYS$OUTPUT without one.

   $STATUS is RMS's success after any run that finished, warnings and
   all, or the RMS status of the file that stopped it.

   DIFFERENCES/SLP writes an update file that turns one file into
   another.

3 Qualifiers
/HEADER

      /HEADER
      /NOHEADER (default)

   Writes the output with each record's line number, insert number,
   and audit trail in a 12-byte record header (VFC records).

/LISTING

      /LISTING[=filespec]
      /NOLISTING (default)

   Writes a listing of the updated file: each line with its number (.1,
   .2, ... for inserted lines), its audit trail, and a -n mark on the
   first line after n deleted ones.  By default the input's name with
   the type .LIS.

/OUTPUT

      /OUTPUT[=filespec] (default)
      /NOOUTPUT

   Names the updated file: by default the input's name and type, a new
   version.  /NOOUTPUT writes none.

/UPDATE

      /UPDATE[=(update-file[,...])]

   Follows the input file and names the update files, by default the
   input's name with the type .UPD; each later one takes its device and
   directory from the one before.  An update file that can't be opened
   is reported, and the others applied.  /UPDATE=SYS$INPUT reads the
   update from the lines after the command in a command procedure.
   Without /UPDATE, the input is copied (and listed) unchanged.

3 Examples

   1.$ EDIT/SUM/LISTING PROG.MAR/UPDATE=FIX1

     Updates PROG.MAR with FIX1.UPD's edits, making a new version of
     PROG.MAR and the listing PROG.LIS.

   2.$ DIFFERENCES/SLP OLD.TXT NEW.TXT
     $ EDIT/SUM/OUTPUT=COPY.TXT OLD.TXT/UPDATE=OLD.DIF

     Makes COPY.TXT, the same as NEW.TXT, from OLD.TXT and the update
     file DIFFERENCES wrote.

2 /SLP

   The same as /SUM: govax's own name for SUMSLP, the one older VMS
   versions used.  (On VMS 7.1, EDIT/SLP runs another utility.)
